# Black-box delivery scenarios

This Playbook takes a project, a confirmed requirements JSON file, and the installed path of this Skill. Its sole deliverable is a reviewed set of black-box delivery acceptance scenarios. A runnable application, application code, devices, test accounts, credentials, third-party integrations, resource preflight, white-box scenarios, and execution evidence are not prerequisites. Never ask Boss for the location of an unimplemented app in this workflow. Never declare application delivery accepted here.

The ledger is `<project>/.delivery-acceptance/`, is Git-ignored, and follows `<skill>/references/data-model.md`. The deterministic stage checker is `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box`. Never put real credentials in the ledger. This is already an active Playbook session: neither Captain nor any role may invoke `run_workflow.py`, `/black-box-scenarios`, or another Playbook from inside it. All role outcomes leave Git HEAD and Git-visible worktree unchanged.

Roles: Analyst creates and repairs scenarios and methods under the ignored ledger. Reviewer independently checks requirement coverage and scenario meaning. Captain reports the terminal result. Reviewer must not modify Analyst's ledger; only Analyst repairs it. The deterministic `script` actor independently reruns the stage checker after a positive Reviewer verdict. Only a zero exit may produce `scenariosReady`. A failed checker returns to Analyst for repair, at most three review rounds. No Tester role and no test execution in this workflow.

## Entry

Analyst verifies that the supplied requirements file contains a confirmed version, source and stable, unique IDs with nonempty text. If absent or ambiguous, return `requirementsGap` with exact `failureDetails`; do not invent or approve requirements. Otherwise run `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py init <project> --requirements <requirements-file>` on first use, or `import-requirements` when the approved source changed. If an initialized ledger already has the same requirements snapshot, reuse it without deleting prior decisions. A changed approved requirements snapshot invalidates prior scenario completeness. Return `ready` after successful initialization or import.

## Design

Analyst maps every confirmed requirement to at least one black-box scenario. Cover normal, exception, boundary, and state-change behavior where relevant. The scenario must be observable from the user or external system boundary and must not claim a particular implementation. Record in `.delivery-acceptance/scenarios.json` with `kind: black_box`, `title`, `requirement_ids`, `expected`, and `method_ids`. Record reusable AI-operated methods in `methods.json` with concrete `preconditions`, `actions`, `evidence_expected`, and `description`; these are plans for later execution, not evidence that execution occurred. Existing test-case references may be linked if actually present. Set `black_box_complete` only after assessing every confirmed ID. Do not set `white_box_complete` without reviewing implemented design/code. Preserve any existing white-box scenarios and resource decisions; a new requirements import already invalidates completeness. Return `done` when the draft is ready for independent review.

## Independent scenario review

Reviewer inspects the requirements snapshot and the scenario and method ledgers. Check every confirmed ID has meaningful coverage, that expected outcomes are observable and testable, and that normal, exceptional, boundary and state-change cases are present where relevant. Identify specific missing or contradictory behavior by requirement and scenario ID. Run `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box` and report its exact requirements and black-box gate results. Return `passed` only if both substantive coverage and the direct checker pass; otherwise return `gaps` with exact `failureDetails`. Do not require the resources, white-box, execution, or review gates to pass at this stage. Do not ask Boss to approve an ordinary scenario list.

After `passed`, the Playbook's `script` actor runs `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box > <project>/.delivery-acceptance/black-box-gate.json`. A zero exit is required. A nonzero exit returns to Analyst for repair if review rounds remain. Analyst repairs only the named gaps in ignored ledger files, preserving confirmed requirement IDs and existing valid scenarios; Reviewer then repeats independent review. If material gaps remain after three rounds, finish `incomplete` with exact gaps. If requirements are unavailable, finish `incomplete` with the exact requirement gap.

## Completion

After the Reviewer and script gate pass, Captain reports `scenariosReady` with a nonempty `response` naming the scenario ledger path, number of black-box scenarios, requirement coverage, and the black-box gate report. Explicitly state that no App behavior was executed or accepted. If a gate fails after allowed repairs, report `incomplete` with exact failure details. A `scenariosReady` result is a successful design deliverable, not full application acceptance. Return a nonempty `response` in either terminal outcome.
